introduction:
Soft trunk robots are designed to navigate sensitive environments, where their entire bodies must deform to operate successfully [17, 13]. Examples include maneuvering in unstructured environments, such as during endoscopy surgeries [5], fuel tank inspections [4], and disaster site inspections [23]. For such applications, a controller needs to track the trunk’s shape accurately. Close to the undeformed state, the trunk’s shape dynamics are linear, but as the trunk deviates from this state, the dynamics tend to become highly nonlinear.

Modeling the nonlinear dynamics of the trunk’s shape is essential to enable shape control via model predictive control (MPC) schemes. Working directly on hardware poses additional challenges, such as no access to the governing equations and only limited access to observable state-space coordinates through data. In such cases, data-driven modeling via invariant manifolds (IMs) that capture the dominant shape dynamics is a robust strategy. Moreover, the dominant dynamics of soft robots are expected to lie on a low-dimensional IM due to an inherent time-scale separation: their intrinsic vibrations decay much faster than their operating speeds.

Specifically, spectral submanifolds (SSMs) and adiabatic SSMs (aSSMs) are the IMs that contain these dominant dynamics. The former models soft robot dynamics locally in the trunk’s workspace [2], whereas the latter models the workspace globally [12]. Both methods have previously been used only to track the end-effector of the trunk, and the aSSM modeling approach has used additional information to avoid obstacles across the full workspace [3]. Recently, [10] has shown that aSSMs can be used to accurately control the end effector and its orientation on a soft trunk simulator. Motivated by these successes, we learn a low-dimensional aSSM-reduced model for a helicoid soft trunk robot and use it in an MPC scheme to demonstrate full-body shape control.

In this work, we contribute by:

learning an aSSM-reduced model of the full body from hardware data and deploying it in an MPC scheme, extending an approach previously applied only to end-effector tracking;

learning an aSSM-reduced model of the full body from hardware data and deploying it in an MPC scheme, extending an approach previously applied only to end-effector tracking;

demonstrating shape control on a range of tasks: dynamic shape tracking, section-by-section shaping and balancing a tennis ball in human-in-the-loop demos. The aSSM-based MPC outperforms the linear baselines by an order of magnitude, and a model-free reinforcement learning (RL) policy trained for 12 hours on hardware fails to complete the balancing task;

redesigning the tendon sleeves of the helicoid trunk platform, which reduces the largest inter-section coupling from 58 °58\text{\,}\mathrm{\SIUnitSymbolDegree} to 6 °6\text{\,}\mathrm{\SIUnitSymbolDegree} of uncommanded deflection and makes the sections independently commandable.

